    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1929616340404322616",
      "url": "https://x.com/MayadeenEnglish/status/1929616340404322616",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/MayadeenEnglish/status/1929616340404322616",
      "text": "Asian manufacturing suffered widespread contraction in May as #US tariffs and trade uncertainty took a heavy toll on exports and production across the region. \n\n#Japan's Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI), a key indicator where readings below 50 signal economic contraction, fell for the 11th consecutive month to 49.4. \n\nSouth Korea's PMI recorded its fourth straight month below 50 at 47.7, marking the steepest decline in output in nearly three years. \n\nVietnam, Indonesia, #Taiwan, and the Philippines all saw factory activity shrink, with Vietnam experiencing its seventh straight month of export order contractions and Indonesia posting the steepest drop in new orders since August 2021. \n\nThe manufacturing downturn reflects the devastating impact of #Trump's tariffs and the breakdown of US-China trade negotiations, which reignited last week when Trump accused China of violating their agreement and doubled steel and aluminum tariffs to 50%. \n\n#China's manufacturing also contracted for a second consecutive month.",
